1D Convolutional Neural Network
===============================

Purpose
-------

Classify an RF IQ window as either a drone or a non-drone signal.

The model receives one IQ window at a time and learns hierarchical temporal
features using one-dimensional convolutional layers.

---

Input
-----

Each sample consists of one normalized IQ window.

Input Shape

    (2, 4096)

Channel 0

    In-phase (I)

Channel 1

    Quadrature (Q)

---

Architecture
------------

Input

↓

Convolution Block 1

↓

Convolution Block 2

↓

Convolution Block 3

↓

Adaptive Average Pooling

↓

Fully Connected Layer

↓

Output

---

Implementation
--------------

Convolution Block 1

* Conv1D (2 → 32)
* Kernel Size = 7
* Batch Normalization
* ReLU
* Max Pooling

Convolution Block 2

* Conv1D (32 → 64)
* Kernel Size = 5
* Batch Normalization
* ReLU
* Max Pooling

Convolution Block 3

* Conv1D (64 → 128)
* Kernel Size = 3
* Batch Normalization
* ReLU
* Max Pooling

Classifier

* Adaptive Average Pooling
* Flatten
* Fully Connected Layer (128 → 1)

---

Output
------

Output Shape

    (Batch Size, 1)

The network returns a single logit for binary classification.

Positive values indicate drone-like RF patterns, while negative values indicate
non-drone RF patterns.

A sigmoid activation is intentionally omitted because the loss function
(BCEWithLogitsLoss) applies it internally.

---

Why Use 1D Convolution?
-----------------------

IQ samples are sequential time-series data.

One-dimensional convolution learns temporal relationships directly from the
signal without requiring conversion into spectrograms or images.

This preserves the original RF information while reducing preprocessing.

---

Why Three Convolution Blocks?
-----------------------------

The first layer learns simple RF characteristics.

The second layer combines lower-level features into more informative temporal
patterns.

The third layer learns higher-level representations useful for distinguishing
drone and non-drone transmissions.

Increasing the number of feature channels allows the network to learn more
complex signal characteristics.

---

Why Kernel Sizes 7, 5 and 3?
----------------------------

The first convolution uses a larger receptive field to capture broader temporal
patterns.

Subsequent layers use smaller kernels to refine increasingly localized and
abstract features.

This progressively reduces the receptive field while increasing feature
complexity.

---

Why Batch Normalization?
------------------------

Batch normalization is applied after every convolution layer.

Benefits include

* Faster convergence.
* Improved training stability.
* Reduced internal covariate shift.
* Support for higher learning rates.

---

Why ReLU?
---------

ReLU introduces non-linearity while remaining computationally efficient.

Benefits include

* Fast computation.
* Sparse activations.
* Reduced vanishing gradient problems.

---

Why Max Pooling?
----------------

Max pooling reduces the temporal dimension after each convolution block.

Benefits include

* Lower computational cost.
* Reduced memory usage.
* Larger effective receptive field.
* Increased robustness to small temporal shifts.

---

Why Adaptive Average Pooling?
-----------------------------

Adaptive Average Pooling converts the final feature map into a fixed-size
representation.

Benefits include

* Removes the need to manually calculate flattened dimensions.
* Simplifies architecture modifications.
* Produces a consistent feature vector for the classifier.

---

Why One Output Neuron?
----------------------

The task is binary classification.

Class Labels

* Drone = 1
* Non-Drone = 0

A single output neuron predicts the probability of the input belonging to the
drone class.

---

Why No Sigmoid Layer?
---------------------

The final layer produces raw logits.

The sigmoid activation is handled internally by BCEWithLogitsLoss during
training.

This approach provides improved numerical stability compared to applying a
separate sigmoid layer.

---

Testing
-------

The implementation is verified by checking

* Forward pass execution.
* Output tensor shape.
* Output tensor data type.
* Numerical stability.
* Compatibility with the RF dataset and DataLoader.
